trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Critical gaps remain: acetaminophen timing/dose and exposure history are needed to assess drug reaction vs. infectious etiology. Since caregiver lacks those details, I’ll pivot to targeted physical examination — the most objective data available now. In an infant with a generalized nonpruritic rash, key exam elements include: rash morphology (macular, papular, confluent?), distribution symmetry, blanching vs. non-blanching (to rule out petechiae/purpura), presence of mucosal involvement (conjunctivae, oropharynx, lips), extremity findings (edema, erythema, desquamation), and lymphadenopathy. These cannot be reliably reported by caregivers and must be assessed clinically.

[TEST] Perform a focused physical examination including:  
- Assessment of rash morphology (e.g., macular, papular, vesicular, purpuric) and blanching with pressure;  
- Evaluation of conjunctival injection (without discharge);  
- Inspection of oral mucosa for erythema, fissuring, or strawberry tongue;  
- Palpation for cervical lymphadenopathy;  
- Examination of hands/feet for edema, erythema, or periungual desquamation.

